  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Light showers end this evening, with low clouds and \nfog lingering into tomorrow morning.\n\nA mature nor'easter continues to spiral across the Northeast this \nafternoon, with the storm center located just south of Long Island. \nWrap around moisture will continue to blanket our region with clouds \nthis afternoon and evening, with a few showers across the North \nCountry. As the low level inverted trough and forcing associated \nwith this now weakening low exits to the east showers will end, but \nlow level moisture will remain abundant. This low level moisture \nwill again bring low stratus and fog to much of our region tonight. \nAreas of fog and low clouds will shroud the hill tops in fog, with \nperhaps a bit of drizzle at times. Patches of fog are likely to be \nforming as well across the Lake Plain. Drier air pushing southward \ntomorrow will aid in eroding fog and low clouds from north to south, \nwith clouds across the inland Southern Tier fading last by early \nTuesday evening. The now mainly clear skies tomorrow night may allow \nfor river valley fog to form, though increasing high clouds from the \nwest may limit the areal extent of the fog. Will include fog with \nthis forecast package update for our eastern areas where fog will be \nmost likely. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warming trend mid-week ahead of the next rain shower \nbearing cold front.\n\nOur wind flow will become southerly by Wednesday of this week, \nallowing a warm autumn airmass to push into our region. In \nconjunction with a ridge aloft, and 850 hPa temperatures rising into \nthe upper teens, surface high temperatures will become some 10 \ndegrees or more above normal, with Thursday the likely warmest day \nthis week. A few sites, especially within the Genesee Valley, will \nmake a run at 80F on Thursday. \n\nThe ensemble suite (ENS, GEFS, and GEPS) continues to highlight \nThursday evening and overnight for the next chance for rain showers. \nActivity is expected to be light upon the front with most areas \naround a tenth of an inch or less...with a spotty two-tenths \npossible across the Southern Tier.",
